---
import site from "../data/site.json";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import DeckFigure from "../components/DeckFigure.astro";
import Citation from "../components/Citation.astro";
import RelatedPublications from "../components/RelatedPublications.astro";
import { asset } from "../data/paths";
---
<Layout title={site.displayTitle} bare>
  <Hero />
  <div class="container visual-overview">
    <section class="section deck-section" id="background" aria-labelledby="background-title">
      <h2 id="background-title">Background</h2>
      <p class="deck-lead">Human-driven and autonomous vehicles will coexist on urban roads during the transition toward automated transportation. This mixed-traffic environment creates new opportunities and challenges for urban traffic management.</p>
      <figure class="rv-spectrum">
        <div class="rv-ends">
          <DeckFigure id="human-vehicle" />
          <DeckFigure id="robot-vehicle" />
        </div>
        <div class="rv-bar" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <figcaption class="rv-labels">
          <span><strong>Human traffic</strong>0% robot vehicles (RVs)</span>
          <span class="rv-mixed"><strong>Mixed traffic</strong></span>
          <span><strong>Robot traffic</strong>100% RVs</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section deck-section text-section" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
      <h2 id="problem-title">Problem</h2>
      <p class="deck-lead">Existing learning-based traffic control methods often focus on individual intersections or simplified traffic settings. Coordinating robot vehicles, human-driven vehicles, and conventional traffic signals across larger road networks remains a challenging problem.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section deck-section text-section" id="rationale" aria-labelledby="rationale-title">
      <h2 id="rationale-title">Our Rationale</h2>
      <p class="deck-lead">Rather than replacing all traffic signals, robot vehicles can regulate traffic at selected intersections while conventional signals continue operating elsewhere. Decentralized multi-agent reinforcement learning offers a way to coordinate traffic through local vehicle decisions.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section deck-section" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-title">
      <h2 id="method-title">Methodology</h2>
      <p class="deck-lead">We develop a decentralized multi-agent reinforcement learning framework for network-scale mixed-traffic control and evaluate it in a 14-intersection road network modeled after Colorado Springs, Colorado.</p>
      <div class="network-compare wide-media">
        <DeckFigure id="prior-work" tag="Prior work" />
        <span class="compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">⇄</span>
        <div class="network-ours">
          <DeckFigure id="network" tag="Ours" />
          <div class="tool-logos"><span>Built with</span><img src={asset("figures/deck/google-earth.webp")} width="480" height="112" alt="Google Earth" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><img src={asset("figures/deck/sumo.webp")} width="200" height="200" alt="SUMO" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="deck-step" id="coordination">
        <h3>01 — Learning-Based Vehicle Coordination</h3>
        <p>Robot vehicles use local traffic information, including queue lengths, waiting times, and intersection occupancy, to make decentralized Stop/Go decisions through a shared reinforcement learning policy.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="method-slide">
        <h4 class="figure-title">Decentralized Reinforcement Learning for Vehicle Coordination</h4>
        <DeckFigure id="method" />
        <p class="figure-caption">Robot vehicles make local Stop/Go decisions using a shared Rainbow DQN policy.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="deck-step" id="hybrid-control">
        <h3>02 — Hybrid Intersection Control</h3>
        <p>We investigate the coexistence of two intersection control strategies: conventional traffic signals and robot-vehicle-based control. Different configurations combine signalized and unsignalized intersections across the road network.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="media-pair wide-media intersection-pair">
        <DeckFigure id="signalized" tag="Signalized (S)" />
        <DeckFigure id="unsignalized" tag="Unsignalized (U)" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section deck-section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <h2 id="results-title">Results</h2>
      <div class="deck-step result-step">
        <h3>Network-Level Traffic Efficiency</h3>
        <p>Under 80% robot-vehicle penetration, a configuration with eight unsignalized and six signalized intersections reduces average waiting time from 6.17 to 5.09 seconds and increases throughput from 454 to 493 vehicles per 500 seconds, compared with fully signalized control.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="result-chart wide-media"><DeckFigure id="results-penetration" describe={false} /></div>
      <p class="figure-caption">Network-level waiting time and throughput under different robot-vehicle penetration rates.</p>
      <div class="deck-step result-step">
        <h3>Intersection-Level Performance</h3>
        <p>Performance varies across intersections and control configurations. Selecting the best-performing configuration separately for each intersection reduces waiting time at 9 of 14 intersections with 50% robot vehicles and 11 of 14 intersections with 80% robot vehicles.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="result-chart wide-media result-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Intersection-level waiting time chart, horizontally scrollable on small screens">
        <div class="result-scroll-inner">
          <p class="chart-labels" aria-hidden="true"><span>50% robot vehicles</span><span>80% robot vehicles</span></p>
          <DeckFigure id="results-intersections" describe={false} />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="figure-caption">Intersection-level waiting time under 50% and 80% robot-vehicle penetration.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section deck-section" id="conclusion" aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">
      <h2 id="conclusion-title">Conclusion</h2>
      <p class="deck-lead">Simulation results demonstrate the potential of decentralized robot-vehicle control to complement conventional traffic signals and improve network-level traffic efficiency. The findings highlight the importance of robot-vehicle penetration and intersection control configurations in future mixed-traffic systems.</p>
    </section>
    <Citation />
    <RelatedPublications />
  </div>
</Layout>
